!FFmpeg - FFmpeg 5.1.10 (LTS) for RISC OS                   riscos17
===================================================================

FFmpeg converts, plays and inspects audio and video. This is FFmpeg
5.1.10, the current release of the 5.1 long-term-support series, built
for ARMv7 machines with NEON: Raspberry Pi 2, 3, 4 and 400, Titanium,
IGEPv5, iMX6 boards, BeagleBoard-xM, Pandaboard. It does not run on a
Pi 1 / Zero or older machines.

It contains three commands:

  ffmpeg    convert between formats, encode, cut, resize, ...
  ffprobe   show what is in a file
  ffplay    play a file in a desktop window (or full screen)

Getting started
---------------
Double-click !FFmpeg: its icon appears on the icon bar. Drop a video (or
sound) file on the icon to play it with ffplay, in a window of its own.
The icon's menu has Info, Convert..., Full screen (tick it to play full
screen), Log and Quit. ffplay's messages (verbose: including the sound
driver it used) go to the file ffplay/log in the scrap directory; Log
opens it.

Converting files
----------------
Click the FFmpeg icon (or choose Convert... from its menu) for the
Convert window. It works from the top down:

  1. Drop a video or sound file on the window. The line under its name
     says what's in it: the picture's codec, size and frame rate
     (and whether it's interlaced), the sound, and how long it is.
  2. Choose what to make from "Convert to":
       For playing here  MP4 up to 720 lines, easy to decode: plays
                         smoothly in Reel on this machine
       MP4, same size    at the picture's own size, good quality
       Smaller file      MP4 up to 480 lines, lower quality
       Sound only: MP3   the sound as a high quality MP3
       Sound only: AAC   the sound as an M4A (AAC) file
     The options under it show what the preset chose; change any of them
     to make your own (the preset then says Custom):
       Format      MP4 or MKV video (H.264 with AAC sound), MP3 or M4A
       Size        the picture's height at most (never made bigger)
       Quality     Best, Good, Normal or Small (a bigger file is better)
       Speed       Fastest, Fast or Balanced (slower makes a smaller
                   file for the same quality)
       Sound       untick for a silent video
       Deinterlace if needed   removes the stripes from interlaced video
       Quick to decode         simpler video that plays smoothly here
       From ... to             only part of the file (e.g. 1:30 to 2:45)
       Options     more ffmpeg options for the new file, as you'd type
                   them on the command line: e.g. -vf hflip (mirror),
                   -r 25 (frame rate), -metadata title=Holiday.
                   They go after the
                   window's own, so they win: -c:v, -crf or -b:a here
                   replace what's chosen above, and -vf replaces the
                   size and deinterlace filters (add them yourself, e.g.
                   -vf scale=-2:480,hflip). Input options (before -i)
                   can't be given here; use From ... to for trimming.
     Options that don't apply are greyed out.
  3. Click Convert. The new file goes next to the original, named after
     it (e.g. holiday_720/mp4 beside holiday/mkv); the name field shows
     the full path. To put it somewhere else, drag the file icon to a
     directory display, as with any RISC OS save box, or type another
     full path and press Return. (If your machine has no icon for the
     new file's type, the file icon shows the Filer's usual "?" icon: it
     still drags.)
  4. A bar shows how far it has got, with how fast it's going (times real
     time) and about how long is left. Stop ends it and deletes the
     unfinished file. When it's done, Play plays the new file and Show
     opens its directory. If ffmpeg couldn't convert it, the window says
     why and Log shows ffmpeg's messages.

Converting takes a while on one core: expect HD video to take longer
than it lasts (the window shows the real speed, e.g. "0.4x real time",
and the time left). Fastest is quickest; Sound only is quick. The desktop
carries on meanwhile: ffmpeg runs in a task window of its own. An
existing file is only replaced after asking. Interactive help (!Help) explains every part of the window.

For everything else, use the commands.
Let the Filer see !FFmpeg (open the directory it is in), or double-click
it once. That sets up the commands. Then open a TaskWindow (Ctrl-F12):

  ffprobe SDFS::RISCOSpi.$.Videos.holiday/mp4
  ffplay  SDFS::RISCOSpi.$.Videos.holiday/mp4
  ffmpeg -i holiday/mp4 -c:v libx264 -crf 23 -c:a aac small/mp4
  ffmpeg -i song/flac -c:a libmp3lame -b:a 192k song/mp3

File names can be RISC OS style or Unix style:
  - full RISC OS names: ADFS::HardDisc4.$.Films.clip/mkv, $.Films.clip/mkv,
    <Obey$Dir>.clip/mp4, Films:clip/mp4;
  - RISC OS names in the current directory (*Dir, or the TaskWindow's):
    holiday/mp4, Films.holiday/mp4, @.holiday/mp4;
  - Unix style: clip.mp4, films/clip.mp4.
The extension after "/" or "." picks the output format. New files get
their filetype from MimeMap.
How a short name is read: the C library (UnixLib) takes a name with no
filing system, disc or directory prefix as a Unix name, so on its own it
would read holiday/mp4 as the file "mp4" in a directory "holiday". Since
riscos12, FFmpeg sees that a name like holiday/mp4 (one "/", a format's
extension after it, nothing before it) is a RISC OS name and opens
@.holiday/mp4. A Unix name with a "/" and no extension (videos/clip) is
still Unix. If in doubt, give the full name, or start it with @. (the
current directory).

ffplay keys: Q or Escape quit, F full screen, P or Space pause,
Left/Right seek 10 s, Up/Down seek 1 min, M mute, 9/0 volume,
W cycle video/waveform/spectrum, S step one frame.
ffplay plays sound through SharedSoundBuffer and StreamManager, John
Duffell's modules (SharedSound, which they use, is part of RISC OS).
Download ssb.zip from Andrew Sellors' RDPClient page:
  https://orac.co.uk/software/rdpclient/rdpclient.html
and merge its !System into yours. !FFmpeg's !Run loads them (so
double-click !FFmpeg once after starting the computer, or add it to Look
at in Configure). John Duffell's own site (now on the Internet Archive)
has more details:
  https://web.archive.org/web/20110920080106/http://www.duffell.riscos.me.uk/

A TaskWindow can't open windows of its own, so when ffplay is started in
one it starts itself again as a separate desktop task (as *WimpTask
would) and the TaskWindow is free at once. Its messages then go to the
file ffplay/log in the scrap directory (<Wimp$ScrapDir>). Set
FFPLAY_NEWTASK to 0 to stop this.

ffplay converts and scales each picture straight into its window, in one
pass. Two system variables change that:
  Set FFPLAY_SCALE bilinear   the scaler when the window is not the
                              video's size: fast_bilinear (the default),
                              bilinear, bicubic, neighbor or area
  Set FFPLAY_RENDERER sdl     SDL's own drawing instead (slower)

Video into EGL and OpenGL
-------------------------
ffmpeg can show video through riscos-mesa's EGL, with no sound:

  ffmpeg -re -i clip/mp4 -map 0:v -f egl "Holiday"
  ffmpeg -re -i clip/mp4 -map 0:v -f egl -fullscreen 1 -

From a TaskWindow a window isn't possible (see ffplay above): use
-fullscreen 1, or start ffmpeg as its own task:
  WimpTask ffmpeg -nostats -re -i clip/mp4 -map 0:v -f egl "Holiday"

Programmers can play videos (with sound) with the reelcore library in
the riscos-ffmpeg devkit, and put them into EGL surfaces and OpenGL
textures with ffegl. docs.EGL
explains both. The FFmpeg-EGL-examples zip has two small programs that
use it: videowin (a player window) and videocube (a video on a spinning
cube).

What is in it
-------------
Decoders for almost everything FFmpeg knows (H.264, HEVC, VP8, VP9, AV1
via dav1d, MPEG-1/2/4, ProRes, AAC, MP3, AC-3, Opus, Vorbis, FLAC, ...).
Encoders: H.264 (x264), MP3 (LAME), Opus, Vorbis, AAC, MPEG-4, MPEG-2,
MJPEG, FLAC, ProRes, FFV1 and more. Network: http, tcp, udp, rtp, rtmp,
hls, and https (and HLS over https) through RISC OS's AcornSSL module
(RISC OS 5.28 or later; !Run loads it):

  ffplay https://example.com/films/clip.mp4
  ffprobe https://example.com/live/stream.m3u8

AcornSSL checks each server's certificate against its own list of
authorities and the server's name. If it can't vouch for one it asks in
the desktop (Accept, Reject or Always); until you answer, ffmpeg waits.
There's nobody to ask outside the desktop, so a certificate AcornSSL
can't check is refused there. If the commands say https needs the
AcornSSL module, double-click !FFmpeg once (its !Run loads it), or
*RMLoad System:Modules.Network.URL.AcornSSL.

Speed
-----
Most of FFmpeg's speed on ARM comes from its hand-written NEON assembly
(and dav1d's and x264's). The PackMan FFmpeg (4.4) is built for ARMv4
without VFP or NEON; this one uses all of it.

RISC OS runs with alignment checking on, and much of that assembly
assumed unaligned loads are allowed (as on Linux). For this build every
such instruction was rewritten so it cannot fault, and FFmpeg's, dav1d's
and x264's own test suites (about 3,000 checks) plus 110 decode/encode
tests all pass under an emulator that traps like RISC OS.

"Bench" (in this directory) measures the speed on your machine: double-
click it. It compares the NEON code with plain C.

On a Raspberry Pi, H.264 can be decoded by the Pi's own video decoder
(the VideoCore) instead of the CPU: ask for it with -c:v h264_vchiq
before the input, for example
  ffmpeg -c:v h264_vchiq -i film/mp4 -c:v libx264 out/mkv
  ffplay -vcodec h264_vchiq film/mp4
Up to 1920x1080, 8-bit 4:2:0 (Baseline, Main and High); 1080p needs
gpu_mem=128 or more in !Boot.Loader.CONFIG/TXT. A stream it can't take
is refused with a message; leave the option out to use the CPU (the
default). Its pictures can differ from the CPU's by 1 in a few colour
values (the VideoCore's own rounding).

On a Raspberry Pi 4 (and 400 and CM4), HEVC can be decoded by the Pi 4's
HEVC block: -c:v hevc_hwdec (ffplay -vcodec hevc_hwdec). 8-bit and
10-bit 4:2:0, up to 4096x4096; -output_8bit 1 gives 8-bit pictures from
10-bit files. Its pictures are exactly those of the CPU's decoder.
Anything it can't take is refused with a message.

Why 5.1 and not 7.x/8.x: 5.1 is the last FFmpeg with NEON code for the
AAC, AC-3, Vorbis and WMA audio decoders on 32-bit ARM (6.0 replaced it
with generic C), and the last whose ffmpeg command doesn't need threads
(RISC OS has one core). It is still maintained (LTS).

Memory
------
Each program is about 31MB and needs about 43MB of application space
(the Boot file sets it). They are bigger than before because they now
include riscos-mesa's EGL and OpenGL for the egl output device. Memory for pictures and sound comes from a dynamic area called
"FFmpeg Heap" (up to 512MB), which goes away when the program quits.

Needs
-----
SharedUnixLibrary 1.16 or later and ARMEABISupport (both in PackMan).

The programs are built with UnixLib 5.0.3.1 (the C library; an
unofficial fork of GCCSDK's UnixLib), so they read
and write files over 2GB, up to 4GB-1 (the largest RISC OS allows; the
filing system must allow them too).

Included: PThreadTicker 0.03, a small module from UnixLib (the C
library the programs are built with). UnixLib switches a program's
threads (ffplay has several) with a timer, and this module holds the
timer's code, so it's always there even while other tasks run. !Run
loads it (a copy merged into !System is used first); without it UnixLib
uses its own copy in the module area, which also works. *Help
PThreadTicker shows it's loaded. It refuses to be killed while a program
uses it. Licence: docs.Licences.PThreadTicker (BSD).

Known limits
------------
- ffplay uses the whole CPU while it plays (Reel, a separate download,
  is a video player that sleeps between pictures).
- Version riscos1's ffplay stopped with "Window Manager is currently in
  use" when started in a TaskWindow; riscos2 started itself as its own
  task, but H.264 video could crash with "abort on data transfer"
  (riscos2 and riscos3). Fixed in riscos4.
- If ffmpeg seems to wait for a key in a TaskWindow, add -nostdin.
- No hardware decoding.

